From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Sat, 22 Oct 94 20:46:37 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-447] Libertarians grabbing a state

The revival of the discussion of Oceania reminds me of a question I posed to
a libertarian many years ago.  Why don't libertarians select a state in the
U.S. with a relatively small population and then move there en masse and
attempt to take over the state government.  One method to do this would
involve an influential libertarian organization announcing the target state
and encouraging members to move there. By concentrating in selected cities
initially it might be possible to take over city governments first.
Alternatively, new towns and cities can be founded or attempts can be made
to secede from existing towns and cities.  Once libertarians are voted into
power they can take actions such as:

  1) abolish public schools
  2) privatize the road system
  3) decriminalize drugs
  4) privatize state and local police forces

Many other policies championed by libertarians can be enacted.  According to
the libertarian world view wonderful developments will accompany these new
policies. There will be a veritable explosion of wealth and happiness.  More
individuals with libertarian mindsets will be attracted to the state and
other individuals who previously favored statist policies will be converted
to libertarianism by the positive transformations underway.

Of course, the statists in the federal government may try to reassert
statist policies.  The federal government may withhold money from a state
which does not enact policies which are approved by the federal policy
makers. This happens even now. But the state government can simply refuse to
take the federal money.  More drastically the federal government could invade
the state. This might occur, for example, if state citizens refuse to pay
federal taxes, engage in market activities such as smuggling, or attempt to
secede.  Perhaps, to be successful the protolibertarian grouping would have
to walk a tightrope between implementing libertarian policies and avoiding
excessively antagonizing other existing states. But the libertarian ideology
suggests that other aggregates of people will join as they see the success
of a well implemented and running libertarian system.

I would guess this has been proposed in the past.  Alaska appears to have a
concentration of libertarians but it is unclear if it is a desirable target
state.  Are libertarians inadequately motivated?  Are there enough
libertarians willing to uproot themselves to attempt to take over a state?
Are libertarians willing to attempt a coordinated voluntary action?


