Subject: [#94-10-448] Brains, Computers and Tinkertoys

Nancy Lebovitz says:
>For what it's worth, I gather that people have assumed that minds
>are like the most complicated machines available--clocks, hydraulic
>systems, switchboards....and now computers are the standard metaphor.
>There's no reason to think that computers are actually a good
>comparison, and some reason (computers and people have wildly
>different strong and weak points) to think that people aren't
>very much like computers.

I have repeatedly seen an argument somewhat similar to the one suggested
above which I will present in an exaggerated manner below and then critique.
(Note, I do not claim Nancy Lebovitz was making the precise argument given
below. I am sure she can speak for herself.)


     People previously thought that minds could be implemented using
     clockwork.  They sure were foolish.  Later people thought minds could be
     implemented with switchboards.  Those people were wrong also.  Ha.
     Indeed, people often think incorrectly that brains can be implemented
     using the most complex currently known machine.  Nowadays, some people
     think minds can be implemented with computers.  These people must be
     making the same mistake as the people in the past. In the future, there
     will probably be an even more complicated machine and some people will
     once again bogusly claim that a brain can be implemented using the new
     complicated machine.

This argument is misleading and deeply flawed. Many people are surprised to
learn that a computer can be built out of clockwork pieces, or hydraulic
systems, or switchboards (with a delay element) or even tinkertoys.  This
fact blunts the whole thrust of the argument given above.

Let us call the viewpoint that computers can be used to implement brains the
``brain mechanist'' position.  People who assert that brains can be
implemented with clockwork, hydraulics, switchboards, or tinkertoys etcetera
are actually usually asserting the same position, i.e., the brain mechanist
position. This follows because computers can be built from any of the basic
elements listed in the previous sentence.  The apparent change in position
over time emphasized in the presented argument given above is misleading
because it is illusory.  Further, it was the apparent change in position
that seemed arbitrary, time dependent and thus unconvincing.  Now, the
recognition of the fact that there has been no real change in position
should make one reconsider the argument.

Computers are very special types of machines.
  Many models for computers and computation have been proposed:
Turing machines, RAM machines, PRAM machines Post correspondence systems,
lambda calculus.

Even the recently introduced quantum computer of Deutsch/Vazirani/Shor etc


Machine with an oracle for the halting problem.
A machine with an oracle for the halting problem 

Strictly speaking to build a Turing machine from tinkertoys ( or clockwork
etc.)  would require an infinite supply of building elements since a Turing
machine has an infinite tape.

Every computer ever actually built has really been an implementation of a
finite state automata and not a true Turing machine.


