Subject: [#94-10-450] (Corrected version of) Brains, Computers and Tinkertoys

I accidentally sent an incomplete draft of this message to the list. Sorry.
Below is the complete version.

Nancy Lebovitz says:
>For what it's worth, I gather that people have assumed that minds
>are like the most complicated machines available--clocks, hydraulic
>systems, switchboards....and now computers are the standard metaphor.
>There's no reason to think that computers are actually a good
>comparison, and some reason (computers and people have wildly
>different strong and weak points) to think that people aren't
>very much like computers.

I have repeatedly seen an argument somewhat similar to the one suggested
above which I will present in an exaggerated manner below and then critique.
(Note, I do not claim Nancy Lebovitz was making the precise argument given
below. I am sure she can speak for herself.)

     People previously thought that minds could be implemented using
     clockwork.  They sure were foolish.  Later people thought minds could be
     implemented with switchboards.  Those people were wrong also.  Ha.
     Indeed, people often think incorrectly that brains can be implemented
     using the most complex currently known machine.  Nowadays, some people
     think minds can be implemented with computers.  These people must be
     making the same mistake as the people in the past. In the future, there
     will probably be an even more complicated machine and some people will
     once again bogusly claim that a brain can be implemented using the new
     complicated machine.

This argument is misleading and deeply flawed. Many people are surprised to
learn that a computer can be built out of clockwork pieces, or hydraulic
systems, or switchboards (with a delay element) or even tinkertoys.  This
fact blunts the whole thrust of the argument given above.

Let us call the viewpoint that computers can be used to implement brains the
``brain mechanist'' position.  People who assert that brains can be
implemented with clockwork, hydraulics, switchboards, or tinkertoys etcetera
are actually usually asserting the same position, i.e., the brain mechanist
position. This follows because computers can be built from any of the basic
elements listed in the previous sentence.  The apparent change in position
over time emphasized in the presented argument given above is misleading
because it is illusory.  Further, it was the apparent change in position
that seemed arbitrary, time dependent and thus unconvincing.  Now, the
recognition of the fact that there has been no real change in position
should make one reconsider the argument.

Computers are very special types of machines.  Many models for computers and
computation have been proposed:  Turing machines, RAM machines, PRAM
machines, Post correspondence systems, lambda calculus etc.  These models
have all been shown to be equivalent in computational power in the sense
that any one of the models can be used to simulate any other model.  Even the
recently introduced quantum computer of Deutsch/Vazirani/Shor etc is no more
powerful than a Turing machine in terms of what it can compute (though it may
compute it more efficiently or faster). The mechanisms (tinkertoys etc)
discussed earlier can be used to build these seemingly universal machines.
In part, because of this seeming universality some believe the brain can be
implemented with a computer. This also means that if a fancy ``supertech''
computer can be used to implement a brain then so can lowly clockwork pieces.
To some this is evidence that the brain can not be implemented with a
computer and to others this is evidence that the brain mechanists were right
from the beginning.

Caveat: There are computational models which are more powerful than Turing
machines.  For example, a Turing Machine with an oracle for the halting
problem.  Further, there are machines more powerful that this:  A Turing
machine with an oracle for the halting problem for machines with an oracle
for the halting problem.  This can be extended to yield a hierarchy of more
powerful machines.  However, currently these machines can not be built using
any standard known physics. Penrose apparently believes our brain uses
currently unknown physical mechanisms to perform computations that can not be
performed with a Turing machine.

Clarification: Strictly speaking to build a Turing machine from
tinkertoys ( or clockwork etc.)  would require an infinite supply of
building elements since a Turing machine has an infinite tape.  Every
computer ever actually built has really been an implementation of a finite
state automata and not a true Turing machine. Brain mechanists also
usually think that brains can be implemented as finite state automata.
For example, Tipler thinks this.       
                                       Gregory Sullivan

